{"authored" [{"question" "q-102809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5x=1-3y \\\\ 3-4y=2x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f5fd037cacc3087bdce6c46f08df39056432b1e05f9b87e69a40066cef54a9d" "basis" 1063 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995674/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-102809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5x+3y=1 \\\\ -2x-4y=-3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}].$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995674/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f5fd037cacc3087bdce6c46f08df39056432b1e05f9b87e69a40066cef54a9d" "basis" 1063 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capt